        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          The Skylab waste management system functioned as a collection, sampling, processing, and storage system for all crew metabolic wastes including feces, urine, and vomit.  The hardware consisted of both fecal and urine collectors (including collection bags, sampling equipment, odor control filters, a blower, and miscellaneous supplies), a waste processor, a freezer, pressure suit drying equipment and a trash air lock.   The urine and fecal collection/processing equipment was housed in the waste management compartment.
<P>
Fecal Collector - Fecal material was collected in a fecal collection bag attached under a form-fitted commode seat.  The bag was permeable to gas but not to liquids.  An electric blower, actuated by the crewmember curing use, provided air flow of 11 cubic feet per minute around the perennial area to carry feces into the collection bag.  After defecation, crewmembers weighed the stool on a specimen mass-measuring device and placed the fecal bag in a waste processor.  After 16 to 20 hours, the fecal residue was removed and stowed for return to earth.
<P>
Waste processor - A six-chamber waste processor dried feces and vomit bags, pressure suit desiccants, and film vault desiccants.  Space vacuum supplemented by heat was the technique chosen for drying.  Drying times were a function of mass to be dried and could range up to 20 hours with vacuum only as the drying source.
<P>
Urine Collector - The urine collector consisted of a urine receiver and hose through which urine passed into a personal urine drawer.  Each urine drawer contained a liquid/gas separator (centrifuge), urine bag, sample container, and refrigeration line to cool the separator.  At the start of urination, the crewmember actuated the liquid/gas separator, creating a positive airflow at one foot per minute.  The airflow separated the liquid fraction of the urine and transferred it into a collection bag inside the urine drawer.  Urine was collected in the bag for 24-hour periods.  A measured amount of lithium chloride was added to the pooling bag before flight to permit calculation of urine volumes based on lithium dilution.  Crewmember also used a gage to measure pooling bag thickness.
<P>
Every 24-hour period, the crewmembers collected 120-milliliter aliquots of urine from the pooling bags and placed the samples in a freezer for return to earth.  The remainder of the urine with the bag was deposited into a urine disposal bag for dumping through the trash air lock, and the separator was flushed to prevent contamination.  The second system, to be used if the urine drawer was inoperative, consisted of a roll-on cuff and bag similar to the Apollo version.  Similar bags were employed for backup and Extra Vehicular Activity (EVA) operations.
<P>
Freezer - Crew urine and blood samples were frozen in the freezer.  The sample temperature was maintained below +27 degrees for 8 hours.
<P>
Pressure Suit Drying Equipment - Pressure suit drying equipment consisted of a blower, hoses, and desiccant bags for removing moisture inside the suit following suit use. Air forced through the desiccant bags dried the suits.
<P>
Air Lock - Trash accumulated from food wrappers, equipment bags, towels, etc. was discarded through an air lock into a large volume tank in the orbital workshop.
<END>
